--TEST--
`$x is $cls` tests the subject against the class a `class<T>` holds, narrows it to `T` on the true edge, and answers `false` for a subject that holds no object — `rule:types/type-test`, `rule:types/class-reference-sites`, `rule:types/narrowing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The one arm of `is` whose right-hand side is a value rather than a type, and
// a `$` is what opens it. The reference carries the descriptor the walk
// compares against, so a class this program only learns at run time asks the
// same question a written name does.
interface Named {
    public function name(): string;
}

class Animal implements Named {
    public function name(): string {
        return "animal";
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    public function name(): string {
        return "dog";
    }
}

class Rock {
}

class<Animal> $animal = Animal::class as class<Animal>;
class<Animal> $dog = Dog::class as class<Animal>;

mixed $pet = new Dog();

// True for the class the reference holds and for every ancestor of the
// subject's own class, exactly as a written name is.
echo ($pet is $dog) as string, "|\n";
echo ($pet is $animal) as string, "|\n";

// False sideways and down: the walk is over the subject's supertype set, never
// an equality on the class the reference names.
mixed $plain = new Animal();
echo ($plain is $dog) as string, "|\n";

// `rule:types/narrowing`: a `class<T>` holds a `T` or an implementor of one, so
// the true edge knows the subject is an `Animal` and the member resolves with
// no conversion.
if ($pet is $animal) {
    echo $pet->name(), "|\n";
}

// A subject whose declared type can hold no object is settled `false` where it
// is written and costs no diagnostic: `is` refuses no left-hand side.
int $n = 7;
echo ($n is $animal) as string, "|\n";

// And a subject holding an object of an unrelated class answers `false` rather
// than throwing, which is the whole reason the test exists.
mixed $rock = new Rock();
echo ($rock is $animal) as string, "|\n";
--EXPECT--
1|
1|
|
dog|
|
|
